{{also|récap}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{etymid|en|from _re-_ plus _cap_ as in top}}
From {{prefix|en|re|cap}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|rē-kăpʹ}}, {{IPA|en|/ɹiːˈkæp/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-recap (verb).wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|æp|s=2}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|++}}

# {{senseid|en|cap off again, v.t.}} To seal (something) again with a cap.
#: {{hyper|en|cap#Verb|cap off#Verb}}
#: {{ux|en|They're planning to '''recap''' those oil wells permanently.}}
# {{senseid|en|retread (a tire), v.t.}} {{lb|en|North America}} To replace the [[worn]] [[tread]] on a [[tire]] by gluing on a new outer portion.
#: {{syn|en|retread#Verb}}
#: {{ux|en|They buy a lot of camelback because they '''recap''' a lot of truck tires for trailer fleets.}}
#* {{quote-book |en |date=1943-11-10 |last=Buckingham |first=Lyle M. |chapter=Distribution of motor-vehicle tires. [Testimony.] |title=Hearings Before the United States Senate Committee on Banking and Currency |publisher=United States Government Printing Office |page=51 |url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Hearings/rH6x3BZLMFAC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22recapping+tires%22&pg=RA5-PA51&printsec=frontcover |passage=In 1942 almost 86,000,000 pounds of camelback—a 25-percent increase over 1941—were made available for '''recapping''' purposes, and the output of this product is continually increasing. Dealer establishments are as busy as beehives. They have about 90 percent of the '''recapping''' equipment. In the testimony before you yesterday one of the proponents of this bill admitted that independent dealers had 88 percent of all the '''recapping''' machinery there is in the United States. We estimate that it is probably about that figure, although we thought it was a little higher, probably 90 percent. In addition to '''recapping''' tires for the general public, they also are doing this work on Government-owned tires, including the military, with the result that the independent dealer has retreaded hundreds and hundreds of thousands of tires, at a substantial profit, during said period.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|work=Kiplinger's Personal Finance|year=1957|month=Aug|volume=11|number=8|title=Recap your old tires?|page=13|passage=Are '''recapped''' tires safe? This is obviously the thing to know before you consider buying recaps.}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-see|retread|retread}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|retreaded tire, noun}} A tire that has had new [[tread]] glued on.
#: {{syn|en|retread#Noun}}
#: {{ux|en|He refused to buy '''recaps''' because he didn't trust them not to come apart on the highway.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|title=Law for Business and Personal Use|isbn=0538445882|author=John Adamson|page=102|passage=As she did so, Stacy noted that the tire had been a '''recap'''—a tire created by gluing a new outer portion of tread onto the worn inner core of old tires.}}

